    \field{abstract}{%
    The issue of how humans and animals perform accurate movements has been
  addressed in various ways. Although this book is promoting concepts stemming
  from dynamical systems theory, other approaches have contributed to the
  understanding of movement as well. Among others, the equilibrium point theory
  and the computational theory deserve to be listed for their contribution to
  this field of research called motor control. In this chapter, using
  single-joint rhythmic movement as an example, I will start first emphasizing
  the respective contributions and drawbacks of each approach. Then I will
  address the issue of parameter selection. Indeed, despite diverging opinions
  about the possible nature of control parameter(s), all three approaches must
  deal with the problem of how adequate parameter(s) to achieve a desired
  movement are selected. At the end of this chapter, I will expose how the
  concept of internal model may offer a solution to this problem.%
    }
